    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Geologic surface interpolations can be augmented by adding physical constraints to available data. Here we outline a method that allows one to constrain surface interpolations for multiple geologic surfaces based on the apparent thickness of the bounded layer. The problem formulation that constrains interpolations is discussed. Numerical instabilities are overcome by regularizing the constrained approach. In simple two line interpolations for small slopes the method can overcome unphysical results of the unconstrianed interpolations. In a field example at Raplee Ridge the capability of this method is illustrated by overcoming interpenetration or negative apparent thickness of two surfaces. The method can be extended to allow for multiple layers and higher gradients in elevation by adding additional terms to the governing biharmonic equation.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
